WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.550
<v Presenter>Okay, so let's get into VPN now</v>

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.490
and let's talk first about site to site VPN

3
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.510
which is a way to call it an AWS managed VPN.

4
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.870
So the idea is that we have our corporate data center

5
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.260
and our VPC and we want to be able to connect them

6
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.480
over the public internet.

7
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.920
So one reason to do it is

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.100
so that they can access each other using private IP

9
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.370
that realistically it will go over the public internet

10
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.950
and it will be more secure, encrypted and so on.

11
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.540
So we have to set up a few things.

12
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.270
First on-premise, you need to set up a software

13
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.110
or a hardware VPN appliance onto your on-premise network.

14
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.750
And then this on-premise VPN

15
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.087
should be accessible using a public IP.

16
00:00:42.087 --> 00:00:45.420
And that makes sense because the VPN is going to go

17
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.920
over the public internet.

18
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.600
So now that we have our VPN appliance

19
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.970
with the public IP in our corporate data center

20
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.820
we are going to do some changes on AWS.

21
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.620
On AWS, you need to set up something called,

22
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.800
a Virtual Private Gateway or VGW

23
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.810
and you attach it to your VPC.

24
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.670
So it's a VPC level resource.

25
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.190
And then you set up a Customer Gateway

26
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.680
and that customer gateway is gonna be configured

27
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.680
to the public IP of the on-premise VPN appliance.

28
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.350
And then you are going to set up a VPN connection,

29
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.150
a site-to-site VPN between on-premise and your AWS.

30
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.550
And what happens is that two VPN connections

31
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.290
will be created for redundancy

32
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.990
and all of them will be encrypted using IPSec.

33
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.270
So in the backend there will be a first tunnel

34
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.860
for your site-to-site VPN

35
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.380
and a second tunnel for redundancy.

36
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.560
So this is the basics of site-to-site VPN.

37
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.760
And, finally, you should know that

38
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.700
if you want to accelerate it to make it work globally

39
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.840
you can use the AWS Global Accelerator to accelerate it

40
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.790
for worldwide networks.

41
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.470
Okay, so next.

42
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:50.940
Let's get into the details

43
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.210
of how the site-to-site VPN setup is being done.

44
00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.760
So we're getting into the route propagation area.

45
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.620
So we have a corporate data center

46
00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.970
and our VPC and both of them have non overlapping CIDRs

47
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.610
and we are creating a site-to-site VPN connection.

48
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.610
So that means that there is a VGW on the VPC site

49
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.400
and a customer gateway on the corporate data center site.

50
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.670
Okay, but now we need to make sure that our instances

51
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.020
in our private subnets can communicate through the VGW.

52
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.690
And for this, we have to set up a route table

53
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.690
on our VPC site at the subnet level to make sure

54
00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.210
that the instance can find its way

55
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.850
to the VGW for the CIDR corresponding

56
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.440
to the corporate data center

57
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.690
and vice versa for the corporate data center

58
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.330
we need to update the on-premise router to make sure

59
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.890
that any IP that corresponds

60
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.390
to the private subnet is going to be routed through the CGW.

61
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.350
So we have two ways of setting up these route tables.

62
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.270
Number one, you can do static routing.

63
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.170
And so it's very easy,

64
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.480
you need to edit the entries into the route tables

65
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.820
of each, the VPC and the corporate data center.

66
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.190
And so on the corporate data center side

67
00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.210
we're going to say anything

68
00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:02.160
that is going to be 10.0.0.1/24 should go through the CGW

69
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.080
which should take the network from the left

70
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.000
and go all the way to the right.

71
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.270
And for either site on the route table

72
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.260
we're saying anything that is 10.3.0.0/20

73
00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.060
should go through the VGW

74
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.760
and we have set up properly our routes.

75
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.990
This is static routing.

76
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.060
So in case things change in the future

77
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.700
we have to go ahead and manually edit our route tables

78
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.440
and that may be complicated

79
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.360
but you can also enable dynamic routing, also called BGP.

80
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.340
So BGP is the Border Gateway Protocol

81
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.400
and it's a protocol that allows your networks

82
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.370
to share the routes automatically with one another.

83
00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.650
And in case the BGP goes over the internet,

84
00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.360
it's called eBGP.

85
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.110
So we don't need BGP to update the routing tables.

86
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:47.940
It will be done for us dynamically.

87
00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:48.900
It's a bit magical.

88
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.080
And so for it to work, then you need to specify the ASN,

89
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.790
the Autonomous System Number of both the CGW and the VGW.

90
00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.183
So the CGW will have a custom ASN

91
00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:02.490
and the VGW will also have a custom ASN.

92
00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.180
And so by enabling BGP, we just need to say yes

93
00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.810
we want dynamic routing, and automatically the route tables

94
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.410
on either of the sides will be automatically updated.

95
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.710
Okay, so next we have a site-to-site VPN connection

96
00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.590
and we want to know if our instance on-premise

97
00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.040
can access internet through our VPC and vice versa.

98
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.420
So let's take an example.

99
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.640
We have our VPC and a public subnets

100
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.290
and we have NAT Gateway that is connected

101
00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.290
to an Internet Gateway that may lead to request

102
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.820
to Google account.

103
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.830
And on the corporate data center side

104
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.010
we have our servers connected to the Customer Gateway

105
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:42.120
and we have established a site-to-site VPN to the VGW.

106
00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.740
And it could also be a direct connect,

107
00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.450
it doesn't really matter for the discussion.

108
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.340
And so we're asking ourselves the question,

109
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.080
can the server on premise connect through the CGW to the VGW

110
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.090
to the NAT Gateway to the Internet Gateway

111
00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.050
to Google account?

112
00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.110
Is it a possible way for us to provide internet access

113
00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.120
to the on premise stuff?

114
00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.940
And the reason is no, it is not okay.

115
00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:03.773
Why?

116
00:05:03.773 --> 00:05:05.580
In this entire architecture

117
00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.860
the NAT Gateway is going to have a restriction

118
00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.830
and the NAT Gateway is saying that any source network

119
00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.140
that comes from the site-to-site VPN

120
00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.440
or direct connect cannot go through the NAT Gateway

121
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.990
into the Internet Gateway into the public internet.

122
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.940
So this setup does not work.

123
00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.250
Okay, next, we have the exact same setup,

124
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.520
but this time instead of using a NAT Gateway

125
00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.860
we're going to use a NAT Instance.

126
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.693
Why?

127
00:05:29.693 --> 00:05:30.840
Because we have a bit more control

128
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.480
over the software on the NAT Instance.

129
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.340
So now we're doing the exact same setup.

130
00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.840
We have our corporate data center

131
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.370
that is connected to our VPC

132
00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.180
either site-to-site VPN or direct connect, and is this okay?

133
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.220
Now, can we access google.com

134
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.597
through our corporate data center?

135
00:05:45.597 --> 00:05:48.840
And the answer is yes, because we manage the software

136
00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.360
on the NAT Instance, we have more control

137
00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.270
and so it is possible for us to create a route

138
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.830
from the corporate data center to the internet through AWS.

139
00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:02.160
Okay, so next, can we have this set up.

140
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.350
So we have our corporate data center

141
00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.960
that has an on-premise NAT that gives us access

142
00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.880
to google.com and we wanna know

143
00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.400
if we can connect our instances

144
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.710
from the private subnet in our VPC

145
00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:16.860
to the internet through our corporate data center.

146
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.920
So, again, we're going to do this time a direct connect

147
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.190
or a site-to-site VPN and the question is,

148
00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.120
does this work or not?

149
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.270
Can the instance connect to the VGW then the CGW

150
00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.370
and then the (indistinct) is not in google.com?

151
00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.960
And the answer is yes.

152
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.060
So this would be an alternative setup

153
00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.960
to using NAT Instances or NAT Gateway on AWS.

154
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.520
And so the reason you would do this

155
00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.380
is that maybe your on-premise NAT already

156
00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.910
does a lot of package filtering,

157
00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.780
maybe it does some rules and some checks on the network.

158
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.240
And so you have a corporate data center rule

159
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.580
to have all the internet traffic go through

160
00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.810
your corporate data center.

161
00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.170
In this case, this would be a valid setup.

162
00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.610
Okay, finally, for VPN.

163
00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.860
We have to discuss about VPN CloudHub.

164
00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.840
So VPN CloudHub is a way

165
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.890
to connect different Customer Gateways together.

166
00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.940
So in this example, we can connect up to 10 Customer Gateway

167
00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.340
for each Virtual Private Gateway.

168
00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.570
So what do we have?

169
00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:16.020
We have our Customers Network with different locations.

170
00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.960
So, for example, New York, Los Angeles, and Miami.

171
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:20.880
And they're gonna be able to connect

172
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.780
and talk to one another through the Virtual Private Gateway

173
00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.510
and the VPN connections that have been established.

174
00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.590
So why do we do this?

175
00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.470
Well, it's a low cost hub-and-spoke model

176
00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:35.100
for primary network in case we wanna connect data centers

177
00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.470
like this or it could be a secondary network

178
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.390
in case our main connections

179
00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.690
between our customer networks are failing.

180
00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.340
So, for example, if you have a direct connection

181
00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.610
between New York and Los Angeles and that connection fails

182
00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.550
then we could fail over to the VPN CloudHub

183
00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.250
that has been set up between the customer network

184
00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.020
on New York and the one in Los Angeles.

185
00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.660
And as you can see, the network goes through

186
00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:01.110
the Virtual Private Gateway into each end customer.

187
00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.640
So what do we get out of it?

188
00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.770
Well, we get a secure communication between the sites

189
00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.040
in case we have indeed set up multiple VPN connections.

190
00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.780
And because it's a VPN connection,

191
00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:11.130
you have to remember everything goes

192
00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.680
over the public internet even though of course

193
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:17.250
your VPN connection is going to be encrypted using IPSec.

194
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.070
And this can be used, as I said, as a failover connection

195
00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.560
between your on-premise locations in case there is

196
00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.500
a network issue between the way you have directly connected

197
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.410
your different on-premise locations.

198
00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.960
What about if you have multiple VPC,

199
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.180
how do we establish VPN connections?

200
00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:34.860
So say you have a corporate data center

201
00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.890
and you have set up a customer gateway and you have one VPC.

202
00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:41.640
In that case, for a VPN based customer,

203
00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.680
AWS recommends creating a separate VPN connection

204
00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:45.300
for each customer VPC.

205
00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.970
So that means that we have to create a VPN connection

206
00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.150
between your VGW and your Customer Gateway here.

207
00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.740
In case we have the second VPC

208
00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.320
we create another site-to-site VPN connection.

209
00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.870
And, finally, in case of a third VPC

210
00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:01.710
we have to create a third site-to-site VPN connection.

211
00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.110
In that case, it can become very, very complicated

212
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.000
if you have many, many different VPCs.

213
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.760
And so AWS will recommend using Direct Connect

214
00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.530
because we can leverage Direct Connect Gateway

215
00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.270
to achieve the exact same thing.

216
00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.880
But we'll see this in the Direct Connect lectures.

217
00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.940
So we have a couple options to fix that problem.

218
00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:19.650
Obviously one of them is Direct Connect Gateway,

219
00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:20.940
we'll discuss it later.

220
00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.590
But one thing we can do is to have a Shared Services VPC.

221
00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.690
In that case, we're going to create a VPN connection

222
00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.970
between our on-premise data center

223
00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.340
and one Shared Services VPC.

224
00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:33.570
So this is represented here,

225
00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:37.338
so we have our Shared Services VPC, and we'll set up a VGW,

226
00:09:37.338 --> 00:09:40.290
a CGW, and a site-to-site VPN between the two.

227
00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.470
And then we're going to replicate the services,

228
00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:47.130
application, databases, everything that can be on-premise

229
00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.520
and we'll replicate it onto the Shared Services VPC.

230
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.760
Or we can deploy proxies in the Shared Services VPC

231
00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.520
to access the on-premise resources.

232
00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.220
So either we can replicate the services

233
00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.620
or we're gonna deploy a fleet of proxies

234
00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.240
that will proxy the request made

235
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.690
into the shared service VPC to the corporate data center.

236
00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.030
So the question is, why do we do this?

237
00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.070
Because now we can do VPC peering

238
00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:14.610
between other VPCs and the shared services VPC.

239
00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:15.480
So here's an example.

240
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.070
We have our first VPC

241
00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.620
and we have created a VPC peering connection

242
00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:21.900
between VPC A and their shared services.

243
00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.270
Same for B and same for C.

244
00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.500
And so what do we get out of it?

245
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.050
I told you that VPC peering is not transitive.

246
00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:31.800
So VPC A cannot access your corporate data center.

247
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.497
VPC B as well cannot access your corporate data center.

248
00:10:34.497 --> 00:10:36.600
And VPC C as well cannot.

249
00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.870
But because we have replicated all their services

250
00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.430
from the corporate data center

251
00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.620
onto the shared service VPC using

252
00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.780
a site-to-site VPN connection,

253
00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.960
then VPC A is able to access

254
00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.480
our services through their shared VPC.

255
00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.730
So either we access the replicated services

256
00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.290
and we directly talk with them

257
00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:57.270
or we talk with the application proxies,

258
00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.120
which by definition are going to proxy the request

259
00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:01.170
onto on-premise.

260
